Let's analyze the structure of the good squares.
The bad intervals are $[L_1, R_1], [L_2, R_2], \dots, [L_M, R_M]$.
Since $R_i < L_{i+1}$, these intervals are disjoint.
The good squares are $[1, L_1-1]$, $[R_1+1, L_2-1]$, $[R_2+1, L_3-1]$, ..., $[R_M+1, N]$.
Actually, the problem says $L_i \leq j \leq R_i$ is bad.
So the good squares are the union of intervals $[1, L_1-1]$, $[R_1+1, L_2-1]$, ..., $[R_M+1, N]$.
