The condition $R_i < N$ implies $R_M < N$, so $[R_M+1, N]$ is non-empty (contains at least $N$).

The problem is equivalent to: can we jump from the interval $[1, L_1-1]$ to $[R_1+1, L_2-1]$, then to $[R_2+1, L_3-1]$, ..., and finally to $[R_M+1, N]$?
Actually, we don't necessarily need to jump *directly* from one interval to the next. We might jump within an interval. But since the intervals are separated by bad squares, any jump that lands in a bad square is invalid.
